<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thirumalai Nayak Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1636">1636</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thirumalai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a> Palace is a 17th-century palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, built around 1636 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirumala-nayak">Tirumala Nayak</a>, ruler of the Madurai Nayak kingdom. Built in a blend of Dravidian and Islamic styles, it is known for its huge columns, domes and stucco work. Only part of the original complex survives, and it is now a protected monument.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thirumalai-nayak-palace.jpg" alt="Thirumalai Nayak Palace" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: எஸ்ஸார் · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madurai_Nayak_Palace_Collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai-nayak-dynasty">Nayaks of Madurai</a> ruled from 1545 until the 1740s, and Tirumala Nayaka, king from 1623 to 1659, raised many buildings in and around his capital. He built the palace, 2 km south-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meenakshi-temple">Meenakshi Amman Temple</a>, as the focal point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>. What survives is the main palace, the king's living quarters; the original complex was about four times larger, 900 ft long by 660 ft wide. Art historians count it a late example of Vijayanagara architecture in its Tamil Nayaka style, which joined Indo-Islamic and Persian influences to the southern Indian tradition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The buildings are of foliated brickwork finished with chunam, a stucco of shell lime mixed with egg white that gave the surfaces a smooth, glossy texture. The interior is richly decorated, the exterior plainer. Beyond the gates lies a central courtyard of about 3,700 square metres with a circular garden, ringed by massive round pillars 82 feet high. The palace had two main parts, the Swarga Vilasam and the Ranga Vilasam, holding the royal residence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, shrine, armoury, bandstand, pond and garden. The Swarga Vilasam, the throne room, is an arcaded octagon under a dome 60 to 70 feet high, carried on stone ribs and columns linked by pointed, scalloped arches.</p><p>A local legend holds that the king hired an Italian architect, and European traders and missionaries did visit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> at the time. Some historians regard theories of European architects or a major Western influence on the design as resting on colonial prejudice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>War damaged much of the palace, and in the 18th century many of its structures were pulled down or built into the houses of the neighbouring streets. British records blamed Tirumala's grandson for stripping it to build his own palace at Tiruchirapalli, though some researchers think local people more probably took the materials. The British used surviving parts as granaries, barracks and a factory, and later as the district court of Madura-Ramnad until 1970. Lord Napier, Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, partly restored it between 1866 and 1872. After independence it was declared a national monument, and light and sound shows tell the story of the Silappathikaram there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thirumalai Nayakkar Mahal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tirumala Nayak, in the 17th century, rebuilt much of the Meenakshi Amman temple and built the <b>Tirumalai Nayak palace</b> in Madurai.</blockquote><a href="/state/madurai-nayak-dynasty">Madurai Nayak dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
